{"name":"Sunset Gold","hex":"#f6c362","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sunset","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The moment each evening when the sun disappears below the western horizon.","The changes in color of the sky before and after sunset."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To phase out."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunset"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sunset","blocks":[{"text":"Sunset is the disappearance of the Sun at the end of the Sun path, below the horizon of the Earth due to its rotation. As viewed from everywhere on Earth, it is a phenomenon that happens approximately once every 24 hours, except in areas close to the poles. The equinox Sun sets due west at the moment of both the spring and autumn equinoxes. As viewed from the Northern Hemisphere, the Sun sets to the northwest in the spring and summer, and to the southwest in the autumn and winter; these seasons are reversed for the Southern Hemisphere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Full_cycle_of_a_Sunset_on_the_high_plains_of_the_Mojave_Desert.jpg/330px-Full_cycle_of_a_Sunset_on_the_high_plains_of_the_Mojave_Desert.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunset"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold"},{"title":"Sunset (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color sunset is a pale tint of orange. It is a representation of the average color of clouds when the sunlight from a sunset is reflected from them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Sunset_2007-1.jpg/330px-Sunset_2007-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunset_(color)"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Cape_may.jpg/500px-Cape_may.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Cape_may.jpg/500px-Cape_may.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sunset over the Delaware Bay at Sunset Beach, New Jersey, U.S., seen through cirrus clouds","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACape_may.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Fiery_sunset_clouds_over_city_silhouette.jpg/500px-Fiery_sunset_clouds_over_city_silhouette.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Fiery_sunset_clouds_over_city_silhouette.jpg/1280px-Fiery_sunset_clouds_over_city_silhouette.jpg